🔧 𝗕𝗮𝘀𝗵 𝗧𝗶𝗽 𝗳𝗼𝗿 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝘀 & 𝗦𝗥𝗘𝘀: 𝘀𝗲𝘁 -𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘀𝗲𝘁 -𝘅 💻

If you write shell scripts — even occasionally — these two little flags can save you hours of debugging and unexpected behavior. Let's talk about:

▶️ 𝘀𝗲𝘁 -𝗲 — "𝙀𝙭𝙞𝙩 𝙤𝙣 𝙚𝙧𝙧𝙤𝙧"

This tells Bash to immediately stop the script if any command exits with a non-zero status.

✅ Useful for:
 • Catching failures early
 • Preventing silent errors
 • Enforcing fail-fast behavior in deployment and CI scripts

🧠 Without it, your script may continue running even after something breaks!

▶️ 𝘀𝗲𝘁 -𝘅 — "𝙎𝙝𝙤𝙬 𝙘𝙤𝙢𝙢𝙖𝙣𝙙𝙨 𝙖𝙨 𝙩𝙝𝙚𝙮 𝙧𝙪𝙣"

This makes Bash print each command (with arguments) before it runs — kind of like a real-time trace log.

✅ Useful for:
 • Debugging scripts
 • Seeing variable expansions
 • Understanding flow in long scripts

